## Module 11 — Logging and `journalctl`

**Target level:** Intermediate  
**Status:** COMPLETED

### Learning objectives
- Inspect systemd-managed application logs.
- Filter logs by service, time, boot, and severity.
- Investigate service failures.
- Understand journal storage and retention.

### Core commands and flags

- `journalctl`
- `-u SERVICE` — filter by unit.
- `-n N` — last N entries.
- `-f` — follow new entries.
- `-b` — current boot.
- `-b -1` — previous boot.
- `--list-boots` — list recorded boots.
- `--since` and `--until` — time filters.
- `-p err` — error priority and more severe messages.
- `-k` — kernel messages.
- `--disk-usage` — journal disk usage.
- `--vacuum-time` — remove archived journal files older than a duration.
- `--vacuum-size` — reduce archived journal storage to a size target.

### Common syntax

```bash
journalctl
sudo journalctl -u nginx
sudo journalctl -u nginx -n 50
sudo journalctl -u nginx -f
journalctl -b
journalctl -b -1
journalctl --list-boots
journalctl --since today
journalctl --since "15:00" --until "16:00"
sudo journalctl -u nginx -p err
journalctl -k
journalctl --disk-usage
sudo journalctl --vacuum-time=7d
sudo journalctl --vacuum-size=500M
```

### Common mistakes
- Looking only at the last log line instead of the relevant time window.
- Assuming all application logs are stored in the systemd journal.
- Deleting logs without understanding retention or incident investigation requirements.
- Assuming journal vacuum commands remove every log file on the machine.

